// Living: the tissues of plants, a leaf, a petal, a stamen or a stem.
//
// A leaf is a few layers of cells under a waxy cuticle. Light that crosses the cuticle is scattered among the cells'
// walls and the air between them, and absorbed by their pigments, chlorophyll above all, until it leaves by either
// face: a leaf reflects and transmits nearly the same spectrum, its transmittance a little under its reflectance in the
// visible (Woolley 1971, "Reflectance and transmittance of light by leaves", Plant Physiology 47:656-662; Jacquemoud &
// Baret 1990, "PROSPECT: a model of leaf optical properties spectra", Remote Sensing of Environment 34:75-91). A petal
// is thinner and holds less pigment, so it passes more of what it reflects. That is why a leaf lit from behind glows
// its own green, and a lily's petal glows white.
//
// The colour is the species' own: Nature draws each organ's colours from the species record into the tissue's vertex
// colour. The tissue's record gives what the colour cannot: the ratio of what it transmits to what it reflects, and the
// cuticle's index and roughness. Light from behind passes through Substrate's two-sided wrap (Sheet.ush).

#pragma once

#include "/Project/Matter/Frame.ush"
#include "/Project/Matter/Optics.ush"
#include "/Project/Matter/Sheet.ush"

/** What a tissue whose reflectance is Albedo transmits, from the ratio its record gives. */
float3 MatterTissueTransmittance(float3 Albedo, float TransmittanceRatio)
{
	return saturate(Albedo * TransmittanceRatio);
}

/** A tissue's face before its history: its species' colour under its cuticle. */
FMatterSurface MatterTissueSurface(float3 Albedo, float CuticleRefractiveIndex, float CuticleRoughness)
{
	return MatterSheetSurface(saturate(Albedo), CuticleRefractiveIndex, CuticleRoughness);
}
